---
// pitomd.com/guides/tiktok — connect a TikTok developer app to Pito Studio
// for video analytics and publishing Shorts. Linked verbatim from Studio's
// own setup wizard as "pitomd.com/guides/tiktok"
// (internal/ui/studio_cockpit_wizard_render.go, wizPhTikTokCreds).
//
// Field labels: Studio's wizard reuses the same generic creds form for
// IGDB/YouTube/TikTok (buildWizardCredsForm in
// internal/ui/studio_cockpit_wizard_forms.go), so the two boxes are titled
// "Client ID" / "Client Secret" even though TikTok's own developer portal
// calls the first one "Client key" — called out explicitly in step 4 below
// so nobody hunts for a field literally named "Client ID" on TikTok's site.
//
// Written for a non-technical reader (owner: "the canonical reader is my
// wife") — plain language first; config.toml gets one aside at the end of
// "How it's handled", never the lead.

import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const TITLE = "Connect TikTok — Pito Studio guides";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "How to create a TikTok developer app and connect it to Pito Studio for video analytics and publishing Shorts — what you need, why it's useful, and how it's kept private.";

const pageUrl = new URL("/guides/tiktok/", Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "HowTo",
      name: TITLE,
      url: pageUrl,
      description: DESCRIPTION,
    },
  ],
};
---

<GuideLayout
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  eyebrow="Analytics & Shorts · TikTok"
  heading="Connect TikTok."
  dek="Connecting TikTok gets you real numbers for what you've posted, and — once TikTok approves it — a way to send new Shorts straight from Studio. It starts with a short, free sign-up on TikTok's own site."
  slug="tiktok"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section id="need">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul class="guide-checklist">
      <li>
        <strong>A TikTok account</strong>, plus a separate sign-up as a TikTok
        developer — that's its own registration, apart from your regular TikTok
        login.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A short note about what your app does.</strong> TikTok asks before
        it lets anything post through it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A bit of patience.</strong> Plan for a few days between applying and
        being approved, not five minutes.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why Studio wants this</h2>
    <p>
      This does for TikTok what the previous guide does for YouTube: real
      numbers for what you've posted, and — once TikTok approves your app —
      sending a finished Short straight from Studio instead of uploading it by
      hand.
    </p>
    <p>
      You don't need this either. Skip it and Studio's editing works exactly the
      same; you'd just export the finished clip and post it yourself through the
      TikTok app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="handled">
    <h2>How it's handled</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout">
      <p>
        The two codes you paste in stay right here on this computer. Studio uses
        them to talk to TikTok directly — there's no Pito server in between,
        because there isn't one.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can disconnect any time by removing the app from your TikTok
        developer account.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technically minded: the codes are kept in Studio's <code
          >config.toml</code
        > on this machine.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="steps">
    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Sign in to TikTok's developer site</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href="https://developers.tiktok.com/">developers.tiktok.com</a
          > and sign in with your TikTok account, or create one.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Register an app</h3>
        <p>
          Create a new app, give it a name, and describe what it does in a
          sentence or two. Being specific — "a personal tool that posts my own
          gameplay videos" rather than something vague — tends to get approved
          faster.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Add the posting feature</h3>
        <p>
          From your app's list of features, add the Content Posting API, plus
          any of the analytics features you want — TikTok lists these
          separately, so you add each one you need. Some stay private or
          draft-only until your app is approved; that's normal for a new app and
          doesn't stop you from getting your codes today.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Copy your two codes</h3>
        <p>
          Once the app exists, its overview page shows a Client key and a Client
          secret — copy both. (TikTok calls the first one "Client key"; Studio's
          own box for it just says "Client ID" — same thing, different name.)
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Paste both into Studio</h3>
        <p>
          In Studio's setup wizard — or later, from Settings — paste the first
          code, move to the next box with <span class="guide-key">Tab</span>,
          paste the second, then press <span class="guide-key">Enter</span> to save.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Good to know</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout guide-callout--limits">
      <p>
        <strong>Approval takes days, not minutes.</strong> TikTok reviews every app
        that asks to post — expect a short wait, unlike the other guides here.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>New apps can only post privately or as drafts.</strong> Until TikTok
        approves yours, anything sent through it lands as private or a draft, so you'll
        still publish it yourself from the TikTok app in the meantime. That restriction
        lifts once you're approved.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Ask for only what you need.</strong> Requesting more features than
        you'll actually use can slow down approval.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>This one's newer than the others.</strong> If a step here doesn't
        quite match what you see on TikTok's site, their own help pages are the more
        current source — their site changes faster than this guide can track.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="done">
    <h2>Back in Studio</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout guide-callout--done">
      <span class="guide-callout__tag">Last step</span>
      <p>
        Open the setup wizard's TikTok step — or Settings, later — paste in your
        two codes from TikTok's overview page, and save.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
